 ## Subtopics
 
-### Zooming Interaction Model
-A camera transformation model for zooming that shifts the center of the object to emphasize user attention on focus parts near the initial button click [S2, unofficial]. It modifies the model-view matrix using the formula $MV' = \text{Translate}(-R \cdot (\vec{P}_{interaction} - \vec{P}_{center})) \cdot MV$, where $MV$ is the original model-view matrix, $MV'$ is the modified model-view matrix, $R$ is the magnification factor, $\vec{P}_{interaction}$ is the position of the initial user interaction, and $\vec{P}_{center}$ is the center of the rendering area [S2, unofficial]. This model is chosen when semantic focus on a specific region during zoom is required, contrasting with standard uniform zooming that scales around the camera origin [S2, unofficial].
+### [Zooming Interaction Model](5857-zooming-interaction-model.md)
+The Zooming Interaction Model is a camera transformation technique used in volume visualization to shift the center of the rendered object during zooming operations. It solves the problem of maintaining user attention on specific focus parts of the volume by dynamically adjusting the model-view matrix based on the initial interaction point. Unlike standard zooming which typically scales uniformly around the view center or object origin, this model emphasizes the region near the initial button click by translating the view. It is a component of the Volume Visualization Camera, which handles scaling, centering, rotation, and translation [Parent].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
+**Time and Memory Complexity:**
+The view matrix computation involves a constant number of matrix multiplications (6) and vector operations, resulting in $O(1)$ time complexity per frame. Memory usage is $O(1)$ for the camera state.
+
